    "url": "https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-01-25-fix-typeerror-unhashable-type-python/view",
    "title": "How to Fix 'TypeError: unhashable type' in Python",
    "snippet": "Feb 26, 2026         Frozen dataclasses with hashable fields are hashable by default @dataclass(frozen=True) class Config: host: str port: int debug: bool = False. config1 = Config(\"localhost\", 5432) config2 = Config(\"localhost\", 5432) #.Use @dataclass(frozen=True) with hashable fields. The key insight is that Python needs hashable objects for dictionary keys and set members because it uses hashing for fast lookups. When you encounter this error, convert your mutable objects to their immutable equivalents.   Summarize",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.pythontutorials.net/blog/how-can-i-make-a-python-dataclass-hashable/",
    "title": "How to Make a Python Dataclass Hashable: Using the id Field as Key...",
    "snippet": "Nov 17, 2025         This error occurs because dataclasses are mutable by default, and mutable objects cannot be hashed. While one solution is to make the dataclass immutable with frozen=True, this isn’t always feasible—you might need to modify fields after creation. In this blog, we’ll explore an alternative approach: using a unique, immutable id field to make dataclasses hashable while retaining mutability. We’ll break down why the error occurs, how hashability works in Python, and provide a step-by-step guide to implementing the id field solution.   Summarize",
    "published": null
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.pythontutorials.net/blog/how-do-i-make-a-python-dataclass-inherit-hash/",
    "title": "How to Make Python Dataclasses Inherit __hash__ Without...",
    "snippet": "Nov 6, 2025       Mutable Dataclasses (default, frozen=False): __hash__ is set to None because mutable objects can change, making their hash values unreliable. Instances cannot be used in hash-based collections. Frozen Dataclasses (frozen=True): __hash__ is automatically generated using the values of all fields. Instances are hashable and immutable. Example: Basic __hash__ Behavior#.   Summarize",
    "published": null
